//? Title: Bayesian additive regression trees for nonlinear regression
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Simulate a nonlinear response where changing a binary setting shifts the
location and size of a jump. Fit a Gaussian BART ensemble to training
observations, score it on an independently generated test set, and plot
posterior fits and uncertainty for each setting.
)

> From weak trees to a regression function

A BART draw contains many shallow trees, each contributing a small
piecewise-constant function. During backfitting, each tree sees the response
with the other trees' current contributions removed. The sampler proposes
a grow or prune, integrates out the candidate leaf means for the acceptance
test, and then draws new leaf means and residual variance.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bart_backfitting.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    datamunge::graphics::Diagram()
        .node("residual", {0, 0}, "Remove the other\ntrees' predictions")
        .node("structure", {5.5, 0}, "Propose a tree\ngrow or prune")
        .node("leaves", {11, 0}, "Sample leaf means")
        .node("ensemble", {16, 0}, "Replace the tree's\ncontribution")
        .edge("residual", "structure")
        .edge("structure", "leaves")
        .edge("leaves", "ensemble")
        .save_svg("build/bart_backfitting.svg");
}
```
